WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.440
If you want to know where you are,
 you can check on your phone.

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:05.760
It listens for signals from whole
 constellations of satellites in orbit,

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.480
and by triangulating all those signals
 and using a lot of maths,

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:11.520
it works out where you are
 and shows you a little dot on a map.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:14.400
But how do the satellites
 know where they are?

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.760
There aren't any landmarks in orbit
 for them to refer to.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:20.520
If satellites are in a high enough orbit,
 well away from the atmosphere,

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.240
then they can predict their position. 

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:24.880
They'll just keep orbiting in
about the same path:

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:26.440
there’s no atmosphere to slow them down.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:28.360
But that's not precise enough. 

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:31.240
There will be distortions
 caused by gravity from the sun and moon,

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:33.360
or even from mountain ranges
down on Earth.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:36.960
So maybe they could use the other
 satellite signals, like GPS.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:42.000
But if they're all relying on each other,
that could steadily drift apart from reality. 

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.880
Maybe they could track the stars
 or the features down on Earth,

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.560
but that's not precise enough either. 

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:48.880
At some point, something down here on

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:52.720
Earth has to look up in the sky
 and work out the position precisely.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:56.840
And one of those somethings is hidden away
 in the countryside of southern England.

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:01.000
It looks like a regular observatory,
 but it's not looking at the stars.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:05.040
- We track manmade satellites
 in various orbits doing various jobs.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:07.040
They all have to have special reflectors 

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:09.600
that enables the light
 to be returned to us.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:14.520
And so we can measure the time of flight,
 and from the time of flight, derive the distance. 

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:17.400
You send out a very short
 pulse of laser light.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:19.760
You do that a thousand times a second.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:23.560
Each of those shots, because you're using
 such a tiny short-pulsed laser,

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:27.720
is 10 picoseconds long,
 which is about 3mm.

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:32.880
That's the limitation on your precision.
 So each shot is about 3-4mm precision. 

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:35.400
- This station works around the clock,
 whenever the sky is clear.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.920
That's why I'm here today:
 there will be some genuine observations

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:41.120
when the clouds break, but right now,
 I'm not actually disrupting anything.

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:44.880
But it does look
 significantly better after dark.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:47.120
- Because it's a laser, it's
 a very specific wavelength.

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:50.760
So you can filter, and that's
 what lets you work in day and night.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:53.760
The main problem with the atmosphere is:
the speed of light in a vacuum

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:54.960
is a very well known constant. 

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.560
The speed of light
 through the atmosphere is variable

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:00.680
according to the mainly the pressure,
but also temperature and humidity.

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:02.640
So we measure those things
 on site, as well. 

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:07.040
There's probably about 35 active stations
 scattered around the globe,

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:09.200
located predominantly
in the northern hemisphere,

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.880
which is a slight problem
from a science point of view.

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:14.040
You'd like them evenly distributed
 all around the globe.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:16.360
There's a couple in Australia
 which are really important

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:19.520
because they're providing a bulk
of the data from southern hemisphere.

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:21.880
Ideally, we'd have a more
 even distribution.

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:24.120
- Of course, as the light fades,
 there's one assumption

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:25.800
that we've been working on
 throughout this video: 

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:28.800
that the earth, the ground
 we're standing on, doesn't move.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.960
And that's not strictly true. 

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:33.520
- In the early days of laser
 ranging, in the 60s,

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:36.000
it was the primary method
 by which they were deriving

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.560
those early measurements of the drift
 between us and North America.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.960
So you can see long-term trends,
 like the ice caps

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:46.800
melting and the redistribution of mass
 from the ice caps to the equator.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:49.640
All the data goes off
 to international databases,

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:51.520
from all the sites all around the world. 

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:53.120
Anyone, any researcher, anywhere 

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:56.720
can go and grab the international data set,
the data from us,

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:59.520
from Australia, from North America,
 from China, from Russia,

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:03.360
and can use that data to derive the orbits
 and do the research they need to do.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:06.560
- Thanks very much to all the team
 at the Space Geodesy Facility.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:09.440
You can find out more about them and
 their work at the link in the description.

